          "content": [
            "# Virtual Memory in 5 Bullet Points\n\n- **What it is:** Virtual memory gives each process its own large, private address space (e.g., \"I have 4 GB of memory\"), even if the physical RAM is smaller. The OS and hardware translate virtual addresses to real physical addresses behind the scenes.\n\n- **Paging:** Memory is split into fixed-size chunks called *pages* (typically 4 KB). Virtual pages are mapped to physical frames via a page table. This lets the OS place pages anywhere in RAM and even swap rarely-used pages out to disk.\n\n- **Page faults:** When your program accesses a page not currently in RAM, the CPU raises a *page fault*. The OS pauses the program, loads the page from disk into RAM, updates the page table, and resumes execution. This is normal and usually invisible to you — but frequent faults (thrashing) kill performance.\n\n- **The TLB (Translation Lookaside Buffer):** A small, super-fast cache in the CPU that stores recent virtual→physical address translations. Without it, every memory access would need an extra page table lookup. A TLB hit is nearly free; a miss means walking the page table (and possibly a page fault).\n\n- **Why you care:** Virtual memory enables memory protection (processes can't stomp on each other), lets you allocate more memory than physical RAM, and simplifies linking/loading — but disk-backed pages are ~100,000x slower than RAM, so access patterns matter (locality is your friend)."
          ],
